1
00:00:00,705 --> 00:00:04,108
SignalPass is an AI introduction assistant.

2
00:00:04,192 --> 00:00:11,556
It helps you find someone relevant in your community, understand why you might connect, and prepare a first message you approve.

3
00:00:13,512 --> 00:00:22,270
A community can be full of talented people, yet it's hard to know who to ask, or what to say. Alice is building a wallet prototype.

4
00:00:22,354 --> 00:00:24,701
She needs usability feedback.

5
00:00:26,672 --> 00:00:30,903
Her agent suggests Bob, who offers wallet-onboarding reviews.

6
00:00:30,975 --> 00:00:34,138
It shows the profile quotes behind the suggestion.

7
00:00:34,198 --> 00:00:37,667
Alice can offer frontend and product feedback in return.

8
00:00:37,751 --> 00:00:39,755
Now there is a reason to connect.

9
00:00:42,672 --> 00:00:45,667
The agent drafts a private introduction.

10
00:00:45,738 --> 00:00:52,469
Alice makes the request concrete: review the first-time wallet setup and confusing approval screens.

11
00:00:52,553 --> 00:00:56,115
She checks the exact message, then decides to send it.

12
00:00:59,672 --> 00:01:01,627
Now Bob opens his inbox.

13
00:01:01,698 --> 00:01:08,364
He can see why Alice chose him, what she offers, and a small next step. He chooses whether to accept.

14
00:01:08,435 --> 00:01:13,403
Each person separately chooses whether to share contact details.

15
00:01:15,632 --> 00:01:20,430
An optional refundable bond on Monad makes commitment verifiable.

16
00:01:20,501 --> 00:01:28,395
Acceptance or decline returns the principal; gas is separate. Alice's introduction used no bond.

17
00:01:30,385 --> 00:01:33,970
The product is live. We're starting with Monad builders.

18
00:01:34,030 --> 00:01:40,287
Our next test is whether real, consenting people find these introductions worth answering.

19
00:01:42,285 --> 00:01:43,649
Built by Bojack.

20
00:01:43,733 --> 00:01:50,147
SignalPass: find someone relevant, give them a reason to reply, and keep the decision yours.
